ladao's incumbency and past survival in a district that now leans slightly Democratic"], "rationale": "The fundamentals and the near-unanimous expert consensus point to a Democratic flip in this slightly Democratic-leaning district, and markets plus late polling reinforce Villegas as the clear favorite. Valadao's incumbency and history of outperforming keep this from being called Safe.", "watch": "California's slow vote count and Valadao's track record of late-breaking crossover support could narrow the margin as ballots are tabulated.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent David Valadao (R) is running."]}, "overview": "California's 22nd District pairs two-term Republican incumbent David Valadao against Democrat Randy Villegas in a Central Valley seat that now leans slightly Democratic after redistricting. Raters across the board treat this as a likely flip: several call it Lean D, one Solid D and one Safe D, with only a couple calling it a pure tossup. Kalshi prices Villegas at 92%, and a late poll reported an 11-point Villegas lead.\n\nMoney is heavily tilted against the incumbent: outside groups have spent about $6.1 million opposing Valadao versus $2.6 million supporting Villegas, though Valadao holds a cash-on-hand edge. Immigration enforcement has been a motivating issue for voters in the district. The forecast expects Villegas to win by roughly seven points, with meaningful uncertainty from thin polling and California's counting process.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7982, "margin": 5.28}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8298, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8404, "margin": 6.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8532, "margin": 6.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.8134, "margin": 6.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.7945, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.8475, "margin": 6.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_de